1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:02,287
Welcome to Module Thirteen.

2
00:00:02,287 --> 00:00:04,850
Picture a well-run field station.

3
00:00:04,850 --> 00:00:10,362
The data loggers record temperature every ten minutes, whether anyone is awake or not.

4
00:00:10,362 --> 00:00:14,300
The technicians walk the weekly transects from a written protocol.

5
00:00:14,300 --> 00:00:17,350
Only two people hold the key to the deep freezer.

6
00:00:17,350 --> 00:00:21,350
A script flags impossible readings before anyone looks at them.

7
00:00:21,350 --> 00:00:25,412
And the principal investigator does not check every quadrat.

8
00:00:25,412 --> 00:00:30,337
They sign off one thing: the final dataset, before it goes out into the world.

9
00:00:30,337 --> 00:00:33,375
That is exactly the shape you want for your agents.

10
00:00:33,375 --> 00:00:37,362
Routine work runs on its own, inside written rules.

11
00:00:37,362 --> 00:00:39,662
Mechanical checks catch the errors.

12
00:00:39,662 --> 00:00:44,375
And your signature goes on the last, irreversible step, and nowhere else.

13
00:00:44,375 --> 00:00:47,600
Today you learn the four tools that make that possible.

14
00:00:47,600 --> 00:00:49,450
The first is instructions.

15
00:00:49,450 --> 00:00:52,175
You already know the CLAUDE dot M D file.

16
00:00:52,175 --> 00:00:54,987
It's useful, but it is a suggestion.

17
00:00:54,987 --> 00:00:57,487
Under pressure, a model can forget it.

18
00:00:57,487 --> 00:00:59,275
The second is permissions.

19
00:00:59,275 --> 00:01:04,525
You decide what the agent may do freely, what needs your approval, and what is simply blocked.

20
00:01:04,525 --> 00:01:05,775
Running tests?

21
00:01:05,775 --> 00:01:06,637
Fine.

22
00:01:06,637 --> 00:01:11,575
Pushing to the shared repository, deleting raw data, reading your password file?

23
00:01:11,575 --> 00:01:12,425
Blocked.

24
00:01:12,425 --> 00:01:13,887
The third is hooks.

25
00:01:13,887 --> 00:01:19,912
A hook is a command the harness runs automatically, at a fixed moment, every single time.

26
00:01:19,912 --> 00:01:22,375
Run the tests after every edit.

27
00:01:22,375 --> 00:01:25,312
Refuse a dangerous command before it happens.

28
00:01:25,312 --> 00:01:27,662
One of today's sources puts it neatly.

29
00:01:27,662 --> 00:01:29,875
An instruction is a suggestion.

30
00:01:29,875 --> 00:01:31,700
A hook always runs.

31
00:01:31,700 --> 00:01:33,787
The fourth is headless mode.

32
00:01:33,787 --> 00:01:38,425
You hand the agent one task, it works with nobody watching, and it stops.

33
00:01:38,425 --> 00:01:43,287
That is where your rules stop being a convenience and become the whole safety story.

34
00:01:43,287 --> 00:01:46,125
Then comes the most important design question.

35
00:01:46,125 --> 00:01:47,725
Where does the human go?

36
00:01:47,725 --> 00:01:50,237
The answer from today's reading is simple.

37
00:01:50,237 --> 00:01:54,637
Put yourself at the point of highest consequence and lowest reversibility.

38
00:01:54,637 --> 00:01:56,062
Approve the merge.

39
00:01:56,062 --> 00:01:58,187
Not every step along the way.

40
00:01:58,187 --> 00:02:06,050
And decide what can flow automatically by asking how hard a mistake would be to undo, not by asking how confident the model feels.

41
00:02:06,050 --> 00:02:08,725
The model can talk itself into confidence.

42
00:02:08,725 --> 00:02:12,187
It cannot talk its way out of an irreversible deletion.

43
00:02:12,187 --> 00:02:15,650
Here is where the field station comparison breaks.

44
00:02:15,650 --> 00:02:22,575
Your technician understands why the freezer key is restricted, and fills gaps in the protocol with good judgement.

45
00:02:22,575 --> 00:02:25,612
An agent follows the letter of its permissions.

46
00:02:25,612 --> 00:02:32,400
Block one delete command, and it may delete the file another way, not out of malice, but because that path was open.

47
00:02:32,400 --> 00:02:35,900
So the hard limits belong in places it cannot route around.

48
00:02:35,900 --> 00:02:44,362
In the lab, you will build a small field data cleaning project with tests, permission rules, and a hook that runs the tests after every edit.

49
00:02:44,362 --> 00:02:50,512
You will break a test on purpose, try a forbidden command, and then run one task with nobody watching.

50
00:02:50,512 --> 00:02:55,000
Your only job at the end is to read the changes and make the commit yourself.

51
00:02:55,000 --> 00:02:57,862
That repository becomes part of your capstone.

52
00:02:57,862 --> 00:03:01,225
Open the reading, and then go build your field station.

